    "known_endpoint_excluded": 20,
    "transform_counts": "y=ln a_m",
    "transform_maximal": "y=ln(1+M_m), likewise for point and interval endpoints",
    "interval_score": "IS80=(u-l)+10*max(l-y,0)+10*max(y-u,0)",
    "WIS": "WIS=(0.5*abs(y-p)+0.1*IS80)/1.5",
    "primary": "0.5*mean(WIS over 8 unknown ln a targets)+0.5*mean(WIS over 6 ln(1+M) targets); smaller is better. Equal family weight; do not treat cells as independent replicates.",
    "secondary": "Report MAE and 80% coverage separately for each family. Peak (smallest maximizer if tied) and first e<1/2: absolute error, IS80, and coverage, reported separately. The shattering prediction is unscored unless a separate full completion-support graph census is supplied.",
    "comparison": "Declare Q or H the finite-forecast winner only if primary scores differ by at least 0.01; otherwise report a tie. No significance claim or asymptotic model selection follows from this single board.",
    "no_tuning": "Keep these two files byte-identical after publication/commit. Any revision must be a separately named, dated file; score this version regardless."
  },
  "asymptotic_closures": {
    "status": "Illustrative heuristic continuations, not inferred asymptotic results and not tested by n=10.",
    "common": "For n>20, with alpha=m/n, define L(n,alpha)=(n/20)*L_model(20,alpha)+n*ln(n/20)*s_model(alpha), using linear interpolation of the finite n=20 forecast. Round exp(L) to a positive integer where L>=0; set counts to zero after the first L<0 on the descending tail. At n<=9 use exact calibration profiles; n=10..20 use the conditional finite rules. This is an explicit convention, not a derived counting law.",
    "Q": "s_Q(alpha)=alpha-2*w_9(alpha). Its first descending zero is 2 and s_Q>0 on (0,2); L_Q(n,2)=n*ln(941580)/20>0. This closure predicts 2n for every sufficiently large n, with a sparse exponential endpoint population.",
    "H": "s_H(alpha)=alpha-.67*alpha^3/3-lambda*max(alpha-1.25,0)^2; lambda=[r-.67*r^3/3]/(r-1.25)^2, r=pi/sqrt(3). This closure keeps the bulk peak near 1/sqrt(.67), then has its first descending zero at r and predicts eventual endpoint extinction.",
    "identifiability": "Either closure can be attached to either finite forecasting rule while preserving every supplied exact datum. A better n=10 score tests transport of the finite terminal correction, not either asymptotic closure."
  },
